+(durations)=
+
+### Durations
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a string such as `90s`, `1h30m` or `1m500ms` to a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A
+bare number is a count of seconds, so `90` is the same as `90s`. The units are `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` and `ms`. They must
+appear from largest to smallest, and each unit may appear at most once. Spaces, signs, fractions and negative values
+are not accepted.
+
+Like ranges, `min` and `max` are inclusive bounds, and either one can be omitted to leave that side unbounded.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    from datetime import timedelta
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(max=timedelta(hours=1)), default="5m")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting for {timeout.total_seconds()} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1m500ms'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=2h'])
+```
+
